===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|s|boyz|pl3qual=eye dialect}}

# {{senseid|en|Q3010}} A [[young]] [[male]] [[human]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Kieran plays football with other '''boys''' in his school.}}
#* {{RQ:Coverdale Bible|book=Zechariah|chapter=8|verse=5|folio=xcix|column=2|passage=The ſtretes of the cite alſo ſhalbe full of yonge '''boyes''' and damſelles, playnge vpon the ſtretes.}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Journal to Stella|letter=XVII|date=7 March 1711|page=371|passage=I find I was mistaken in the sex, it is a '''boy'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Byron Childe Harold|canto=II|stanza=XXIII|page=72|passage=Ah! happy years! once more who would not be a '''boy'''?}}
## {{lb|en|particularly}} A [[male]] [[child]] or [[adolescent]], as [[distinguished]] from an [[infant]] or [[adult]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1876|author=Frances Eliza Millett Notley|title=The Kiddle-a-Wink|chapter=A Tale of Love|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qhcCAAAAQAAJ&printsec=frontcover|page=168
|passage="He is not quite a baby, Alfred," said Ellen, "though he is only a big stupid '''boy'''. We have made him miserable enough. Let us leave him alone."}}
# {{lb|en|diminutive|especially|with a possessive}} A [[son]] of [[any]] [[age]].
#* {{RQ:Scott Lay of the Last Minstrel|stanza=II|page=94|passage=Low as that tide has ebbed with me, / It still reflects to memory’s eye / The hour, my brave, my only '''boy''', / Fell by the side of great Dundee.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|writer={{w|Gordon Smith}}|actor={{w|Jonathan Banks}}|role=Michael Ehrmantraut
|title={{w|Better Call Saul}}|season=1|number=6|episode={{w|Five-O (Better Call Saul)|Five-O}}|year=2015|time=38:10
|text=I tried. I tried. But he wouldn't listen. My '''boy''' was stubborn. My '''boy''' was strong. And he was gonna get himself killed. Now I told him, I told him I did it too. That I was like Hoffman, getting by, and that's what you heard that night: me talking him down, him kicking and screaming until the fight went out of him. He put me up on a pedestal. And I had to show him that I was down in the gutter with the rest of them. Broke my '''boy'''. I broke my '''boy'''.}}
# {{lb|en|affectionate|diminutive}} A [[male]] [[human]] [[younger]] than the [[speaker]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|sometimes mildly derogatory}} A [[male]] [[human]] of any age, as opposed to a "girl" ([[female]] human of any age).
#: {{ux|en|[['''boys'''' club]]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1889|year_published=1899|author={{w|Rudyard Kipling}}|title={{w|Under the Deodars}}|chapter=The Education of Otis Yeere|location= Boston|publisher=The Greenock Press|page=10|text="My dear girl, what has ''he'' done?" said Mrs. Mallowe, sweetly. It is noticeable that ladies of a certain age call each other "dear girl," just as commissioners of twenty-eight years' standing address their equals in the Civil List as "my '''boy'''."}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=10 Dec 89|volume=17|number=22|journal=Gay Community News|page=9|author=Tatiana Schreiber;Liz Galst;Emma Stonebridge|title=Drawing The Line: Lesbian Sex And Art: What Do You Like, How Do You Decide?|text=It opened up a whole range [of feelings]. Some of them we were prepared for ― like for some women to feel some of the images were anti-feminist {{...}} One woman wrote, "I can see the '''boys''' have taught you well." "Macho B.S.," someone else wrote.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[male]] of [[low]] [[station]], (''especially as pejorative'') a [[worthless]] [[male]], a [[wretch]]; a [[mean]] and [[dishonest]] [[male]], a [[knave]]. {{defdate|14th|17th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear Q1|act=I|scene=iv|sig=&#91;C4&#93;|verso=1|page=32|text=Do’ſt thou call mee foole '''boy'''?}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare|and|usually|offensive|_|outside some Commonwealth nations}} A [[male]] [[servant]], [[slave]], [[assistant]], or [[employee]], {{defdate|from 14th c.}} ''particularly:''
#* {{RQ:Conrad Heart of Darkness|page=211|passage=He allowed his ‘'''boy'''’ - an overfed young negro from the coast - to treat the white men, under his very eyes, with provoking insolence.}}
#* {{RQ:Pynchon Gravity's Rainbow|page=37|passage=“Why does he go out and pinch all his dogs in person? He’s an administrator, isn’t he? Wouldn’t he hire a '''boy''' or something?”<br>“We call them ‘staff,’” Roger replies, {{...}}}}
## A [[younger]] [[such]] [[worker]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1721|author=Penelope Aubin|title=The Life of Madam de Beaumount|section=ii, 36
|passage=I resolved to continue in the Cave, with my two Servants, my Maid, and a '''Boy''', whom I had brought from France.}}
## {{lb|en|historical|or|offensive}} A [[non-]][[white]] [[male]] [[servant]] [[regardless]] of [[age]], {{defdate|from 17th c.}} ''particularly as a form of address''.
##* '''1625''', W. Hawkins in Samuel Purchas, ''Hakluytus Posthumus or Purchas his Pilgrimes'', Vol. I, iii, vii, 211:
##*: My '''Boy''' Stephen Grauener.
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1834|author=Edward Markham|title=New Zealand or Recollections of It|section=72
|passage=They picked out two of the strongest of the '''Boys''' (as they call the Men) about the place.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1876|author=Ebenezer Thorne|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=IdkNAAAAQAAJ|title=The Queen of the Colonies, or, Queensland as I Knew It|section=58
|passage=The blacks who work on a station or farm are always, like the blacks in the Southern States, called '''boys'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1887|author=Harriet W. Daly|title=Digging, Squatting, and Pioneering Life in the Northern Territory of South Australia|page=233|passage=From a domestic point of view the advent of the Chinese was a decided blessing, for, instead of the European ladies of the settlement having to do all their own work, they were able to employ a proper staff of Chinese '''boys'''.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=May 13 1907|location=N.Y|journal=Evening Post|section=6
|passage=[In Shanghai,] The register clerk assigns you to a room, and instead of ‘Front!’ he shouts ‘'''Boy'''!’}}
##* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1959|year_published=1972|author=w:Anthony Burgess|title=Beds in the East|series=The Malayan Trilogy|page=521|passage=He thrust his head into the aisle. "'''Boy'''!" A Chinese in a white coat responded listlessly. "What will you have? Beer?"}}
##* {{quote-text|en|date=February 5 1960|title=Northern Territory News|section=5/5
|passage=Aborigine Wally... described himself as ‘number one '''boy'''’ at the station.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[male]] [[camp follower]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1572|translator=John Sadler|author=Flavius Vegetius Renatus|title=Foure Bookes... Contayninge a Plaine Forme, and Perfect Knowledge of Martiall Policye...|section=iii, vii
|passage=If any water be rough and [[boisterous|boysterous]], or the [[channel|chanell]] [[very]]e [[broad]]e, it [[many]]e times [[drowns|drowneth]] the carriages and the '''boyes''' and [[now]]e and then [[slothful|slouthfull]] and lyther [[soldiers|souldiours]].}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5 Q1|act=IV|scene=vii|sig=&#91;E4&#93;|verso=1|page=46|text=[[God's blood|Godes plud]] [[kill|kil]] the '''boyes''' and the [[luggage|lugyge]],<br>Tis the [[arrantest|arrants]] [[piece|peece]] of [[knavery|knauery]]{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|now|offensive}} [[any|Any]] [[non-]][[white]] [[male]], [[regardless]] of [[age]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* '''1812''', Anne Plumptre translating Hinrich<!--sic--> Lichtenstein, ''Travels in Southern Africa, in the Years 1803, 1804, 1805, and 1806'', Vol. I, i, viii, 119:
#*: A [[Hottentot]]... expects to be called by his name if addressed by any one who knows it; and by those to whom it is not known he expects to be called Hottentot... or '''boy'''.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1888|author=Louis Diston Powles|title=Land of Pink Pearl, or Recollections of Life in the Bahamas|section=66
|passage=Every [[darky]], however old, is a '''boy'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 8 1973|journal=Black Panther|section=7/2
|passage=[In Alabama,] Guards still use the term ‘'''boy'''’ to refer to Black prisoners.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author=w:Bert Newton; w:Mohammed Ali|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xWKyDGGptA4|title=The Logie Awards
|passage=''BN:'' [''repeating a catchphrase''] I like the '''boy'''.<br>''MA:'' [''to hostile audience''] Hold it, hold it, hold it. Easy. Did you say ‘Roy’ or ‘'''boy'''’?<br>''BN:'' ‘I like the '''boy'''’. There's nothing wrong with saying that... Hang on, hang on, hang on... I'll change religion, I'll do anything for ya, I don't bloody care... What's wrong with saying that? ‘I like the '''boy'''’?<br>''MA:'' '''Boy'''...<br>''BN:'' I mean, I like the man. I'm sorry, Muhammad.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|especially|with a possessive}} A male [[friend]].
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1980|album=[[w:One-Trick Pony (album)|One-Trick Pony]]|author=w:Paul Simon|title=w:Late in the Evening|text=The next thing I remember, I am walking down the street / I'm feeling all right, I'm with my '''boys''', I'm with my troops, yeah.}}
# {{lb|en|BDSM}} A male [[submissive]].
# A [[male]] non-human [[animal]], ''especially, in affectionate address,'' a [[male]] [[pet]], especially a [[dog]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|C'mere, '''boy'''! Good '''boy'''! Who's a good '''boy'''?}}
#: {{ux|en|Are you getting a '''boy''' cat or a girl cat?}}
# {{lb|en|historical|military}} A [[former]] [[low]] [[rank]] of [[various]] [[armed]] [[service]]s; a [[holder]] of [[this]] [[rank]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=May 6 1841|journal=Times|location=London|section=5/4
|passage=Wounded... 1 '''Boy''', 1st class, severely.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=April 30 1963|journal=Times|location=London|section=16/2
|passage=He joined the Navy as a '''boy''' second class in 1898.}}
# {{lb|en|US|slang|uncountable}} [[heroin|Heroin]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2021|author=Tim Weber|title=Heroin: the Ripple Effect
|passage={{...}} drove by a corner, saw what I thought—no, what I knew—were dealers and asked if they knew where I could get some '''boy'''.}}
# {{lb|en|somewhat|childish}} A [[male]] (tree, gene, etc).
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1950|title=Pageant
|passage=Are there “'''boy'''” trees and “girl” trees? Yes. A number of species, among them the yew, holly and date-bearing palm, have their male and female flowers on different trees. The male holly, for instance, must be planted fairly close to the female&nbsp;...}}
#* '''1970''' [earlier '''1963'''], Helen V. Wilson, Helen Van Pelt, ''Helen Van Pelt's African Violets'', Dutton Adult ({{ISBN|9780801538582}}):
#*: Of the 100 percent total, 25 will have two girl genes, 50 will have one '''boy''' and one girl gene, and 25 will have two '''boy''' genes.

